  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 AM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery shows a shortwave trough over northern Missouri, \nslowly moving eastward. It's progress is being hampered by the the \ndeep upper low/ Nor'Easter over the eastern US that is stuck within \na broad upper ridge. This is close to a Rex block setup but not \nquite there just yet and it seems unlikely to evolve into a true \nblocking pattern. Regardless, it is still slowing the progression of \nwaves through the middle of the country. There is another wave, \npotentially a subtle internal PV anomaly, leading to showers and \nstorms in southeastern Kansas as of 07Z. with the near blocking \npattern to the east, this wave will meander over/through the \nsouthern half of the forecast area today, leading to continued \nisolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms, mainly south/ east \nof I-35. While we'll see greater instability, especially across\neastern Kansas and far western Missouri, shear remains weak so\nsevere weather is not anticipated. Precipitation chances should\nfinally start to wind down by 03Z to 06Z Monday, leading to dry\nconditions for Monday into Tuesday. \n\nFocus then turns to the potential for widespread heavy rainfall and \nflooding for the middle to later portions of the work week,\nTuesday night through mainly Thursday. From a synoptic \nperspective, this pattern flashes heavy rainfall. In the upper-\nlevels, a potent shortwave trough will be moving into the \nSouthwest. This will help draw copious amounts of mid and upper-\nlevel moisture from the Pacific into the central US. This \nmoisture is will be the remnants of what is currently Hurricane \nPolo. In the lower-levels, a broad high pressure area over the \neastern US will lead to a strong moisture transport from the \nGulf northward into eastern Kansas and western Missouri. This \ncombination of rich lower-to-mid-level moisture and upper-level\nmoisture will lead to very anomalous precipitable water values.\nIn fact, these values are 3+ standard deviations above normal \nfor this time of year, above the 99.5 percentile for a large \nportion of the area, and at the max values on the NAEFS \npercentiles and GEFS M-climate. Ensemble guidance shows roughly \na 10-20% chance for at least four inches of rain for the run \nending at 7PM Friday. This jumps to 20-30%+ for total rain of at\nleast 3 inches through the same time frame. With all that said,\nthere remains a fair amount of uncertainty on where the \nheaviest rains will actually setup. That will largely depend on \nwhere the strongest forcing sets up as the shortwave ejects into\nthe Plains Wednesday into Thursday as the system takes on \ndistinctive mid-lattitude cyclone characteristics, with a \nnotable deformation area, a warm frontal region extending\neastward, and cold front extending southward. Each of these \nareas will provide a focus for heavy rainfall and placement of \nthese features will be key to defining the areas with the \ngreatest flooding potential.",
